WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.316
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:08.165
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:12.409
<c.asks>And today, we want to see what the
state of a qubit actually looks like.</c>

00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.987
Last time, we ended on two
columns of amplitudes that

00:00:16.987 --> 00:00:19.743
differ by nothing but a minus
sign in the bottom entry.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.214
And if you square the entries
of either one, you get

00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:26.264
exactly the same thing, a
half and a half, fifty-fifty.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.375
<c.asks>So if I measure them, I
can't tell them apart.</c>

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.408
You can't tell them apart, at least
not by measuring like this, because

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:36.481
either way every result looks like
the flip of a fair coin.

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:38.161
<c.asks>Then are they
the same state?</c>

00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:41.601
Well... no. They're not
the same state, and seeing

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:44.326
why is really the whole
point of this section.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.048
What we need is a picture
of a state where that

00:00:47.048 --> 00:00:49.102
minus sign is something
you can actually see.

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:53.300
Let's start by drawing two
axes, a horizontal one for the

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.275
outcome zero and a vertical
one for the outcome one.

00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:00.428
A column of two amplitudes is
then just a point in this plane,

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:03.931
so we can draw an arrow from the
origin out to it, like this.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.153
Now remember the rule from
last time, that the squares

00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:09.305
of the two amplitudes have
to add up to one.

00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:13.152
<c.asks>That's Pythagoras. So the
arrow has length one.</c>

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.589
It is Pythagoras, and it means that
every state of a qubit is an arrow of

00:01:17.589 --> 00:01:21.689
length one, so as the state changes,
its tip just moves around this circle.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.677
Physicists write an arrow
like this with an angle

00:01:24.677 --> 00:01:27.087
bracket around its label,
and they call it a ket.

00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.745
The arrow lying along the
zero axis is called ket zero,

00:01:30.745 --> 00:01:34.050
and the one pointing straight
up the other axis is ket one.

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:38.029
Any other arrow you can build by
taking some amount of ket zero,

00:01:38.029 --> 00:01:41.101
then some amount of ket one, and
placing them tip to tail.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.561
And when both of those amounts are non-
zero, the state is called a superposition.

00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:50.813
<c.asks>I've heard that one. It means the qubit
is zero and one at the same time, right?</c>

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.102
Well, zero and one at the same
time is how it's usually put,

00:01:55.102 --> 00:01:58.413
and honestly I think that hides
the picture. There is only one

00:01:58.413 --> 00:02:01.453
arrow here, and it just isn't
pointing along either axis.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.055
Now let's go back to those
two columns from the start,

00:02:05.055 --> 00:02:07.310
and draw the first one as
an arrow, like this.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.733
It sits exactly halfway between
the two axes, and this particular

00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:15.298
arrow comes up so often that it
has its own name, ket plus.

00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:18.839
The second column is the same
except for that minus sign on the

00:02:18.839 --> 00:02:21.858
bottom entry, so before I draw
it, where do you think it points?

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:25.759
<c.asks>Same distance along zero,
but down instead of up?</c>

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.732
Down instead of up, exactly, because
the bottom entry is the one that tells

00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:35.114
you how far to go along the ket one
axis. This one is called ket minus.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.816
Now look over at the bars for
these two states, and you'll

00:02:38.816 --> 00:02:41.714
see they are exactly the
same, fifty-fifty both times.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.655
But the arrows themselves could
hardly be more different.

00:02:45.733 --> 00:02:47.596
<c.asks>Wait, they're at
a right angle.</c>

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:51.321
Yes, they are at a right angle, and
that's really the whole point. A

00:02:51.321 --> 00:02:54.810
probability can never be negative,
so a random bit has nowhere to keep

00:02:54.809 --> 00:02:58.298
this difference, while a qubit keeps
it in the direction of its arrow.

00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:02.704
<c.asks>Is there a way to compute that
right angle, without drawing it?</c>

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.474
Definitely, there is a way to
compute it, and it comes from asking

00:03:06.474 --> 00:03:10.174
how much of one arrow lies along the
other, like the shadow you see here.

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:14.205
When the amplitudes are real numbers,
you find that by multiplying the

00:03:14.205 --> 00:03:17.245
matching entries of the two columns
and adding up the results.

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:21.843
So try it yourself for ket plus and ket
minus, before I fill in the answer.

00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:26.351
<c.asks>A half minus
a half. Zero.</c>

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.292
You're right, a half minus a half, so
the overlap is exactly zero, and that

00:03:31.292 --> 00:03:35.697
is what a right angle means. Two states
like that are called orthogonal.

00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:39.827
The way we write an overlap is with
a full bracket, where the left half

00:03:39.827 --> 00:03:42.981
is called a bra and the right half
is the ket you already know.

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:46.428
And if you take the overlap
of an arrow with itself, you

00:03:46.428 --> 00:03:49.289
get its length squared, which
for a state is always one.

00:03:50.233 --> 00:03:54.272
Now, two arrows of length one at a
right angle is all that a pair of axes

00:03:54.272 --> 00:03:58.199
ever was, so nothing stops us from
using these two as our axes instead.

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:02.348
In other words, ket plus and ket
minus are just as good a basis as ket

00:04:02.348 --> 00:04:06.137
zero and ket one, and I can turn the
whole picture so that they sit level.

00:04:06.967 --> 00:04:10.205
Notice that the arrow itself hasn't
moved. Only its coordinates have

00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:13.685
changed, and each new coordinate is
its overlap with one of the new axes.

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:16.370
So here's a quick check
for you. In this new

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.473
basis, what are the
coordinates of ket zero?

00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:23.937
<c.asks>It sits halfway between them.
One over root two, twice.</c>

00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:27.326
Halfway between them, yes,
one over root two both times,

00:04:27.326 --> 00:04:30.221
and the squares still add to
one, because the length of an

00:04:30.221 --> 00:04:32.710
arrow doesn't care which axes
you measure it with.

00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:36.098
Okay, so I owe you a
confession. Every arrow

00:04:36.098 --> 00:04:38.964
I've drawn so far had real
numbers for its entries.

00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:42.483
In general the amplitudes can
be complex numbers, and then

00:04:42.483 --> 00:04:45.589
each entry is itself a little
arrow, sitting in its own disc.

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:48.621
<c.asks>So a size, and a
direction it's turned.</c>

00:04:48.967 --> 00:04:51.427
A size and a turn, that's
exactly it, and here is one

00:04:51.427 --> 00:04:54.455
example that we'll carry with us
through the rest of the lecture.

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:58.427
Its first entry has size one over
root three and isn't turned at

00:04:58.427 --> 00:05:01.929
all, while the second is longer,
and turned by forty-five degrees.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.580
If you square just the sizes
you get one third and two

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.718
thirds, which add up to one,
so this really is a state.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.253
And that minus sign from before
turns out to be the simplest

00:05:12.253 --> 00:05:14.806
case of all, a pointer turned
exactly halfway round.

00:05:15.733 --> 00:05:18.680
There's one last detail. With
complex entries, the bra undoes

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.627
each turn before multiplying,
which is what keeps the overlap

00:05:21.627 --> 00:05:24.132
of an arrow with itself equal
to its length squared.

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:29.419
So a state carries more than a pair of
probabilities. It also carries the signs

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:34.050
and the turns of its amplitudes, which is
why these two arrows are different states.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.402
<c.asks>But measuring only
showed me the bars.</c>

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.569
It only showed you the bars, and
that's the question we're left

00:05:40.569 --> 00:05:43.837
with. How much of the arrow does a
measurement actually let us see?

00:05:44.433 --> 00:05:47.067
That's what we'll take up next,
in section one point three.
